--TEST--
Every way a sealed entry fails to open is the same miss and never an error — another ring, another name, and a lifetime already over all answer `null` — while a ring that is wrong in itself throws instead — `rule:security/protocol-roster`, `rule:concurrency/cross-request-state-is-explicit`
--FILE--
<?nvs
// covers: Core\Cache\Store::getSecret
// A caller that could tell "wrong ring" from "tampered" apart would learn
// something about the ring from an entry it cannot read, so `getSecret` gives
// one answer for all of it. That answer being a miss rather than a throw is
// what lets a rotated ring re-fetch instead of failing the request.
secret bytes $mine = Core\Crypto::generateKey();
secret bytes $theirs = Core\Crypto::generateKey();
array<secret bytes> $ring = [$mine];
array<secret bytes> $other = [$theirs];

secret string $token = "hunter2";

var $cache = Core\Cache::memory();
$cache->putSecret("token", $token, 1h, $ring);

// A ring that sealed nothing here is a miss: `null`, and not the ciphertext
// handed back under a key that cannot open it.
echo "a ring that sealed nothing here is a miss: ",
    $cache->getSecret("token", $other) == null ? "null" : "something", "\n";

// The bound asserted on the other side, so the line above is the ring and not
// the entry having never been written.
echo "and its own ring still opens it: ",
    ($cache->getSecret("token", $ring) ?? "") == $token ? "yes" : "no", "\n";

// The entry's name is sealed in as well as used to find it, so a secret is not
// readable under a second name even by the ring that sealed it.
echo "a name nothing sealed under is a miss: ",
    $cache->getSecret("never-written", $ring) == null ? "null" : "something", "\n";

// The lifetime is sealed in beside the value as an expiry of its own, so an
// entry the tier was slow to forget is still past it.
$cache->putSecret("over", $token, 0s, $ring);
echo "a lifetime already over is a miss: ",
    $cache->getSecret("over", $ring) == null ? "null" : "something", "\n";

// And the one thing that is not a miss: a ring that cannot key anything is the
// program's bug, so it is the exception class a program's bugs get rather than
// a `null` a caller would read as "no entry".
array<secret bytes> $empty = [];
try {
    $cache->getSecret("token", $empty);
    echo "an empty ring read something", "\n";
} catch (LogicError $noKeys) {
    echo "an empty ring is a program bug and not a miss", "\n";
}
--EXPECT--
a ring that sealed nothing here is a miss: null
and its own ring still opens it: yes
a name nothing sealed under is a miss: null
a lifetime already over is a miss: null
an empty ring is a program bug and not a miss
